th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/asymptotic-equivalence.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/1a-Math-I-Calculus/asymptotic-equivalence.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_asymptotic_equivalence_3eb0a15f_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does $f(x)\\sim g(x)$ as $x\\to a$ mean?","options":["$\\lim_{x\\to a}\\bigl(f(x)-g(x)\\bigr)=0$","$\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}$ exists and is non-zero","$\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=1$","$\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to a}g(x)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"569b9f2c3dd6f4ec1fdbdce5","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_asymptotic_equivalence_3eb0a15f_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does asymptotic equivalence compare?","options":["The values of $f$ and $g$ at the limiting point $a$ itself","Relative behaviour or growth rates near a point or infinity","The derivatives of $f$ and $g$ at the limiting point $a$","The overall size of $f$ and $g$ across the whole of their domain"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"76500c22a653c236dce717ca","time_limit":35},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_asymptotic_equivalence_3eb0a15f_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which logarithmic asymptotic equivalence is valid as $x\\to 0$?","options":["$\\ln(1+x)\\sim x$ as $x\\to 0$","$\\ln x\\sim x$ as $x\\to 0$","$\\mathrm{e}^x\\sim x$ as $x\\to 0$","$1+x\\sim x$ as $x\\to 0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c346f991c0dab512de7d9f7a","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_asymptotic_equivalence_3eb0a15f_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which hidden issue must be avoided when using the ratio $\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}$?","options":["$f$ and $g$ must both tend to $0$ at the limiting point $a$","$g$ must be non-zero at the limiting point $a$ itself","$f$ and $g$ must both be bounded on a neighbourhood of $a$","The quotient must make sense near the limiting point"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"58e63c480ebf1507939fd4a9","time_limit":40},{"id":"repair_asymptotic_equivalence_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=x+x^2$ and $g(x)=x$. What is the correct conclusion as $x\\to0$?","options":["$f(x)\\not\\sim g(x)$, because $f(x)\\neq g(x)$ for all nearby $x\\neq0$.","$f(x)\\sim g(x)$, because $\\dfrac{f(x)}{g(x)}=1+x\\to1$.","$f(x)\\sim g(x)$, because $f(0)=g(0)$.","$f(x)\\not\\sim g(x)$, because both functions tend to $0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3b21327a15a7cf26185aeb2e","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_asymptotic_equivalence_3eb0a15f_003_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=\\mathrm{e}^{2x}-1$. Which function is asymptotically equivalent to $f(x)$ as $x\\to0$?","options":["$2x$","$x$","$1+2x$","$\\mathrm{e}^{2x}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"06356c3d0ec188ca3d2468d3","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_asymptotic_equivalence_3eb0a15f_006_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"As $x\\to0$ we have $\\sin x\\sim x$. In which limit does replacing $\\sin x$ by $x$ give a wrong value?","options":["$\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin x-x}{x^3}$","$\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\sin x}{x(1+x)}$","$\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{(\\sin x)^2}{1-\\cos x}$","$\\lim_{x\\to0}\\frac{\\mathrm{e}^x\\sin x}{\\ln(1+x)}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1c35a717bcd099ad3ae5f4d2","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_asymptotic_equivalence_3eb0a15f_007_curated20261027_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Suppose $f(x)\\sim g(x)$ as $x\\to a$. What can be said about the reversed ratio $\\frac{g(x)}{f(x)}$?","options":["It is defined for all $x\\neq a$ close to $a$ and tends to $1$","It tends to $1$ only if $f$ and $g$ have a common finite limit at $a$","It need not be defined near $a$, because $f$ may have zeros arbitrarily close to $a$","Its limit need not exist, because only the ratio $\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}$ is controlled"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6a8ce3179f0ca101bfc234f9","time_limit":45},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_asymptotic_equivalence_3eb0a15f_008_curated20261027_004","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Suppose $f(x)\\sim g(x)$ and $g(x)\\sim h(x)$ as $x\\to a$. Which statement is guaranteed?","options":["$f(x)\\sim h(x)$ as $x\\to a$","$f(x)-h(x)\\to0$ as $x\\to a$","$\\lim_{x\\to a}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to a}h(x)$ exist and are equal","$f(x)=h(x)$ for all $x\\neq a$ close enough to $a$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a9e0364604cf15989b526e3d","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_asymptotic_equivalence_3eb0a15f_topup20261028_001","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=x^2+x$ and $g(x)=x^2$, so that $f(x)\\sim g(x)$ as $x\\to+\\infty$. Which pair of functions is NOT asymptotically equivalent as $x\\to+\\infty$?","options":["$\\sqrt{f(x)}$ and $\\sqrt{g(x)}$","$\\mathrm{e}^{f(x)}$ and $\\mathrm{e}^{g(x)}$","$\\frac{1}{f(x)}$ and $\\frac{1}{g(x)}$","$x\\,f(x)$ and $x^3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5abc8678c66e32fb47d5064d","time_limit":40},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_asymptotic_equivalence_3eb0a15f_topup20261028_002","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Let $f(x)=1+x$ and $g(x)=1+2x$. Then $f(x)\\sim g(x)$ as $x\\to0$, since $\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\to1$. Does $\\ln f(x)\\sim\\ln g(x)$ hold as $x\\to0$?","options":["Yes; $\\ln$ preserves equivalence of positive functions","Yes; $\\ln$ is continuous, so both logarithms tend to $0$","No; $f$ and $g$ tend to $1$, and the log ratio tends to $\\frac{1}{2}$","No; the quotient $\\frac{\\ln f(x)}{\\ln g(x)}$ is undefined near $0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"339c43581791aac806803f23","time_limit":45},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_asymptotic_equivalence_3eb0a15f_topup20261028_003","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f(x)=\\cos x$ and $g(x)=1+x$. Is $f(x)\\sim g(x)$ as $x\\to0$?","options":["Yes, since $\\frac{\\cos x}{1+x}\\to\\frac{1}{1}=1$","No, since $f'(0)=0$ but $g'(0)=1$","No, since both would have to tend to $0$","No, since $\\cos x\\sim1-\\frac{x^2}{2}$ instead"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"74bcea99f53f918ee5bdf9a7","time_limit":30}]}}